###Setting up a proportion

Corresponding sides of similar triangles are proportional, so we can set up and solve a proportion:

$\dfrac{RG}{JG}=\dfrac{GJ}{GT}$

Side $\overline{RG}$ is equal to $\overline{GT}+\blueE{\overline{TR}}$ and we need to find the length of $\blueE{\overline{TR}}$.

We picked side $\overline{JG}$ because it corresponds to side $\overline{RG}$.

Then we picked another pair of corresponding sides where we already knew the measurements.

$\begin{array}{rcl}
&\triangle JRG
\\
{\text{hypotenuse}}
&\overbrace{\underbrace{\!\!\!\!\!\left\{ 
\dfrac{GT+\blueE{TR}}{JG}=\dfrac{GJ}{GT}
\right\}\!\!\!\!\!}}
&{\text{short} \atop \text{leg}}
\\
&\triangle TJG
\end{array}$
